cbor::structs<std::set<std::uint8_t>>::encode({3, 1});                          \f[I]// 258([1, 3])\f[R]
cbor::structs<std::array<std::byte, 2>>::encode({std::byte{1}, std::byte{2}});  \f[I]// 64(h\(aq0102\(aq)\f[R]
cbor::structs<std::chrono::sys_seconds>::encode(std::chrono::sys_seconds{std::chrono::seconds{1700000000}});
\f[I]// 1(1700000000)\f[R]
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]decode\f[R] takes each of these types only under its own tag.
A typed array takes only the little\-endian tag of its element, and a
\f[CR]std::array\f[R] only its own count.
So a \f[CR]std::vector\f[R] or a \f[CR]std::array\f[R] of a number type
does not take a plain array: it gives \f[CR]error::incorrect_type\f[R].
A \f[CR]std::set\f[R] takes the items of tag 258 in any order; an item
that comes twice gives \f[CR]error::duplicate_key\f[R].
\f[CR]std::chrono::sys_seconds\f[R] also takes a float under tag 1 when
its value is a whole number; any other float gives
\f[CR]error::number_out_of_range\f[R].
.PP
Where the standard library has \f[CR]std::chrono::parse\f[R]
(libstdc++), a \f[CR]std::chrono::sys_time\f[R] also takes the RFC 3339
date\-time of tag 0, and a \f[CR]std::chrono::year_month_day\f[R] the
RFC 3339 full\-date of tag 1004.
The letters \f[CR]T\f[R] and \f[CR]Z\f[R] may be lower case.
A fraction of a second needs a \f[CR]sys_time\f[R] that holds it, for
example \f[CR]sys_time<milliseconds>\f[R]; a \f[CR]sys_seconds\f[R]
gives \f[CR]error::incorrect_type\f[R] for it.
Second 60 gives \f[CR]error::incorrect_type\f[R].
libc++ has no \f[CR]std::chrono::parse\f[R], so tags 0 and 1004 give
\f[CR]error::incorrect_type\f[R] there; a program that needs them brings
a type of its own.
.PP
An enum with no fixed underlying type does not compile in
\f[CR]decode\f[R], because some values of its underlying type are not
values of the enum.
.PP
\f[CR]encode\f[R] writes a NaN in the shortest width that holds its bits
exactly (RFC 8949 4.1).
A NaN whose low 42 bits of a binary64 are zero is a binary16, one whose
low 29 bits are zero is a binary32, and any other NaN is a binary64.
A \f[CR]float\f[R] NaN keeps its own bits.
\f[CR]decode\f[R] gives the NaN back with the same bits.
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// a double NaN with the bits 7ff8000000000000 is f9 7e00\f[R]
\f[I]// a double NaN with the bits 7ff8000020000000 is fa 7fc00001\f[R]
\f[I]// a double NaN with the bits 7ff8000000000001 is fb 7ff8000000000001\f[R]
.EE
.PP
A \f[CR]float\f[R] takes a binary16, binary32 or binary64 item.
A binary64 item that a \f[CR]float\f[R] cannot hold exactly gives
\f[CR]error::number_out_of_range\f[R].
A binary64 NaN whose low 29 bits are not zero is such an item.
.PP
\f[CR]encode\f[R] throws \f[CR]std::logic_error\f[R] for a
\f[CR]std::chrono::year_month_day\f[R] that is not a valid date.
When an allocation is refused, \f[CR]encode\f[R] throws
\f[CR]std::bad_alloc\f[R], and \f[CR]decode\f[R] returns
\f[CR]error::not_enough_memory\f[R].
.SS 4. Structs and keys
A struct is a map.
Each public data member is one entry.
The keys come in the order of RFC 8949 4.2.1: sorted by their encoded
bytes.
.PP
An aggregate with no description is read by the names of its members:
through reflection on a build with \f[CR]\-freflection\f[R], through
Boost.PFR on other builds.
It takes no annotation, so it gives the same bytes on every build:
\f[CR][[=cbor::key(\(dqx\(dq)]]\f[R] and \f[CR][[=cbor::tag(n)]]\f[R] on
a type that \f[CR]structs\f[R] reads do not compile.
An aggregate with a base class needs a description.
cbor::schema reads the same description.
.PP
A description gives the tag and the members of a struct or a class.
\f[CR]CBOR_DEFINE_TYPE(T, tag, members...)\f[R] writes it at global
scope.
The macro writes this specialization of
\f[CR]cbor::describe_members<T>\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]template\f[R] <>
\f[B]struct\f[R] cbor::describe_members<T> {
    static \f[B]constexpr\f[R] std::optional<cbor::tag> tag = \f[I]/* tag */\f[R];
    static \f[B]constexpr\f[R] std::tuple members{\f[I]/* members... */\f[R]};
};
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]tag\f[R] is a \f[CR]std::optional<cbor::tag>\f[R], and
\f[CR]members\f[R] is a \f[CR]std::tuple\f[R] of \f[CR]cbor::member\f[R]
entries.
The tag \f[CR]cbor::untagged\f[R] gives a map with no tag, an untagged
message in the words of RFC 9052 2 (SenML, CTAP2, the COSE header).
A decode of such a type refuses a tag in front of the map with
\f[CR]error::incorrect_type\f[R].
.PP
