.IP \(bu 2
The description stands outside every namespace, after the type and after
the description of each struct that the type holds.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]CBOR_DEFINE_TYPE_MEMBERS(T, tag, m...)\f[R] takes up to 343 names.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]CBOR_DEFINE_TYPE\f[R], \f[CR]CBOR_DEFINE_TYPE_MEMBERS\f[R] and
\f[CR]CBOR_MEMBER\f[R] take a type alias for a type whose name holds a
comma, because the preprocessor splits the arguments of a macro at each
comma.
.IP \(bu 2
An entry names a public data member of the type or of a public base.
.IP \(bu 2
On a build with \f[CR]\-freflection\f[R], the entries are exactly the
public data members of the type and of its public bases.
Another list does not compile.
.IP \(bu 2
A member that comes twice, two members with one key, a tag that the
library takes for a standard type (for example 1, 258 or a tag of RFC
8746) and a tag that the IANA registry calls always invalid do not
compile.
.IP \(bu 2
A description is checked where \f[CR]encode\f[R], \f[CR]decode\f[R] or
the size of the type is compiled.
A description that nothing uses is not checked.
.IP \(bu 2
No C++ literal holds \-2\(ha63.
The key \-2\(ha63 is
\f[CR]std::numeric_limits<std::int64_t>::min()\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
A described class needs a default constructor for
\f[CR]decode(bytes)\f[R].
A class with no default constructor decodes into an object that the
caller gives: \f[CR]decode(owner, T(...))\f[R].
.IP
.EX
\f[B]class\f[R] account {
\f[B]public\f[R]:
    account() = \f[B]default\f[R];
    std::string user;
    std::uint8_t level = 0;
\f[B]private\f[R]:
    std::string password;
};

CBOR_DEFINE_TYPE(account, cbor::tag{160061}, CBOR_MEMBER(account, user), cbor::member{1, &account::level});

\f[I]// an account with user \(dqann\(dq and level 2 is 160061({1: 2, \(dquser\(dq: \(dqann\(dq})\f[R]

\f[B]struct\f[R] senml {          \f[I]// RFC 8428 6: n is 0, v is 2\f[R]
    std::string n;
    double v;
};

CBOR_DEFINE_TYPE(senml, cbor::untagged, cbor::member{0, &senml::n}, cbor::member{2, &senml::v});

\f[I]// senml{\(dqt\(dq, 1.5} is {0: \(dqt\(dq, 2: 1.5}\f[R]
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]decode\f[R] reads the entries of a map in any order:
.IP \(bu 2
a key that no member has: the entry is skipped, whatever the type of the
key, an array, a map and a tag included;
.IP \(bu 2
a key of a member that comes twice: \f[CR]error::duplicate_key\f[R] (RFC
8949 5.6); a \f[CR]std::map\f[R], a \f[CR]std::unordered_map\f[R] and a
\f[CR]std::flat_map\f[R] give the same error on a key that comes twice;
.IP \(bu 2
a missing key of a member that is not optional:
\f[CR]error::key_not_found\f[R].
.SS 5. Tags
A described struct carries the tag of its description (section 4).
\f[CR]cbor::tagged_item<N, E>\f[R] (RFC 8949 3.4, \(dqtagged data
item\(dq) writes tag N on the item of E, for content that is not a
struct, for example the array of COSE_Sign1 (RFC 9052 4.2).
Over a struct it does not compile.
A struct that carries the annotation \f[CR][[=cbor::tag(n)]]\f[R] does
not compile in \f[CR]structs\f[R].
.IP
.EX
\f[B]struct\f[R] person { std::string name; std::int64_t age; };

CBOR_DEFINE_TYPE_MEMBERS(person, cbor::tag{1800}, name, age);

cbor::structs<person>::encode({\(dqann\(dq, 30});
\f[I]// 1800({\(dqage\(dq: 30, \(dqname\(dq: \(dqann\(dq})\f[R]
.EE
.SS 6. Variants
\f[CR]decode\f[R] gives a \f[CR]std::variant\f[R] the first alternative
that accepts the item.
An integer alternative accepts an integer that it can hold.
\f[CR]encode\f[R] writes the alternative that the variant holds.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]using\f[R] id = std::variant<std::uint8_t, std::int64_t, std::string>;
.EE
.PP
