\f[CR]encode\f[R] writes no shared value.
.SS 8. A memory budget
The form with a target reads into the containers of the target.
A target of \f[CR]std::pmr\f[R] containers sets the memory resource of
every element.
When the resource refuses, \f[CR]decode\f[R] returns
\f[CR]error::not_enough_memory\f[R].
.IP
.EX
\f[B]using\f[R] texts = std::pmr::vector<std::pmr::string>;
\f[B]auto\f[R] const owner = std::make_shared<std::string const>(
    *cbor::structs<std::vector<std::string>>::encode({std::string(40, \(aqa\(aq), std::string(40, \(aqb\(aq)}));
std::array<std::byte, 64> small{};
std::pmr::monotonic_buffer_resource budget(small.data(), small.size(), std::pmr::null_memory_resource());
cbor::structs<texts>::decode(owner, texts(&budget));   \f[I]// not_enough_memory\f[R]
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]decode\f[R] does not reserve memory for a count that the input
states.
Each element is made when it is read.
basics.md, section 6, describes the memory of every API.
